  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cole gave them quality innings and the offense responded with one run. Tied through six until Toronto broke it open with a pair, leaving the Yankees chasing a game they should have won by the ninth.",
    "what_this_means": "The pattern reasserted itself in the clearest way possible. Cole allowed one earned run across six innings. The kind of outing that wins ballgames. But the lineup managed only five hits and left four runners on base without converting any of them into runs. Trent Grisham was the only batter who delivered, going 2-for-4 with a home run that kept them within reach. The damage came in the sixth, when Toronto scored twice and the Yankees never answered. This is the cost of leaving runners stranded: eventually the other team capitalizes and you're standing at the plate needing what your pitching already bought you. Four at-bats with runners in scoring position. Nothing to show for it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Cam Schlittler takes the mound tonight against Toronto with a 2.33 ERA over his last four starts, facing a Braydon Fisher lineup that's been quiet enough to make this a pitcher's game if the bats can find a way.",
